This project has two codelines, both private TypeScript ES-module packages with identical tooling:

- mocka at /home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a — source in src/, tests in test/. Scripts: `vitest run` (test), `tsc --noEmit` (build/type-check). No lint configured. The ticket MOCK3-1 concerns `fareFor` in src/fares.ts, which currently gates the senior concession with `rider.age > 65` instead of `>= 65`, so a rider aged exactly 65 is charged the base fare. The existing test suite (test/fares.test.ts) has no case for age 65. `totalFor` delegates to `fareFor`, so any boundary change propagates through it.
- mockb at /home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b — source in src/, tests in test/. Scripts: `vitest run` (test), `tsc --noEmit` (build/type-check). No lint configured. The ticket MOCK3-2 concerns `formatStops` in src/schedule.ts, which loops `i < stops.length - 1` and therefore omits the final stop from the rendered string. The existing test suite (test/schedule.test.ts) asserts the arrow separator and the first stop name but never asserts the last stop appears.

The implementer role (typescript-bugfix-engineer) spans both codelines. The two investigator roles are codeline-specific: mocka-investigator reads mocka only, mockb-investigator reads mockb only. When reviewing a brief, check that it assigns work to the correct codeline and does not, for example, ask the mocka investigator to inspect mockb source or vice versa.

Both codelines use `strict: true` in tsconfig.json with `noEmit: true`, so type errors will surface from `tsc --noEmit` but no build artefacts are produced. Tests are run with `vitest run` (single pass, not watch mode).

